  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1131 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nA weak shortwave trough continues to slide across the Great Plains \ntonight into Wednesday morning. A few showers have kicked off across \nnorthern Kansas and are slowly making their way up into southeastern \nNebraska. They will be fighting some dry air, so not super confident \nin much measurable precipitation reaching the ground in many \nlocations. A few sprinkles or a light shower may be possible (20% \nchance), mainly for those along and south of a line from David City \nto Wahoo to Council Bluffs.\n\nThursday, a mid-level ridge builds over the Central High Plains into \nthe Dakotas, shifting over the Great Plains by Friday. Expect \nafternoon high temperatures to climb into the mid to upper 70s each \nday, with a few locations approaching 80. Friday will be a day to \nmonitor for changes, which could result in some fire weather \nconcerns. Minimum relative humidity values will be mainly in the mid-\n30s to mid-40s. There will be some pockets of lower RH values (upper \n20s to mid-30s) along the Kansas Nebraska border. Wind gusts in this \narea may approach 25mph. The areas of stronger winds are anticipated \nto stay near the Nebraska/South Dakota border where RH values will \nbe higher. That being said we do have some areas with drier fuels. \nShould a fire spark, there may be some localized problems; however, \nwidespread fire weather concerns are not currently expected.\n\nSaturday, low pressure moves across Saskatchewan and Manitoba with \nan attendant cold front sweeping across Nebraska and the Dakotas. \nExpect a few showers and possibly a rumble of thunder across our \nnorthern Nebraska counties Friday evening as the front starts to \nmove through. Better chances for showers are expected Saturday into \nSunday. Additional shower activity is expected Monday as a mid-level \ntrough drops into the region from the north. \n\nSaturday high temperatures will be dependent on location as the \nfront moves through. Areas in northern Nebraska may struggle to \nreach the 70s, while areas near the Kansas/Nebraska/Missouri border \nmay approach 80 degrees. Cooler air settles in Sunday and Monday \nwith highs in the 50s and 60s. Lows will be primarily in the 40s. \nTuesday morning, portions of northern Nebraska may fall into the mid \nto upper 30s.",
